.TH SD 3
.SH NAME
sd \- the card or disk as a file, with named ranges
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #S /dev

.B /dev/sdcard
.B /dev/sdctl
.BI /dev/ name
\&...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sd
is the native kernel's whole contribution to storage: a device that can be
read and written as a range of bytes.
It knows nothing of partitions and nothing of file systems.
A partition table is data written on the card, not a property of the
hardware, so reading one is a program's job;
.IR dossrv (4)
reads FAT out of a file this device serves and is an ordinary program
doing so.
.PP
The device is one storage unit of 512-byte blocks: the SD card on the
Raspberry Pi 3B+, a virtio block device under QEMU's
.B virt
machine.
It refuses to attach when there is none, so on a machine with no card
the bind fails and
.B /dev
is unchanged; the kernel binds it after probing for one.
.PP
.B Sdcard
is the whole device.
Reads and writes need not be block aligned: the ends of a request go
through a one-block buffer, and a write to part of a block reads the block,
patches it and writes it back.
Reads and writes beyond the end are truncated to it.
.PP
.B Sdctl
lists and names ranges.
Reading it gives the size and the ranges that have been named, one per line:
.IP
.EX
blocks 62333952
part sd0 2048 524288
.EE
.PP
It accepts one control message:
.TP
.BI part " name start nblocks"
Name the
.I nblocks
blocks beginning at block
.I start
as
.IR name ,
which then appears as
.BI /dev/ name
and behaves as
.B sdcard
does, confined to that range.
Naming an existing
.I name
redefines it.
There may be eight; a name may be 31 characters.
Nothing is checked against the ranges already named: two may overlap.
.PP
.IR Osinit (8)
is what normally writes these: it reads the master boot record from
.BR sdcard ,
names each primary partition
.BR sd0 ", " sd1 ,
\&... and hands the first FAT one to
.IR dossrv .
.SH EXAMPLE
Name the card's first partition by hand and mount it:
.IP
.EX
echo part boot 2048 524288 > /dev/sdctl
dossrv -f /dev/boot -m /n/dos
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/port/devsd.c
.br
.B /os/bcm2837/sdmmc.c
(the card, on either of the BCM2837's controllers)
.br
.B /os/virtio/blkvirtio.c
(a virtio block device)
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR dossrv (4),
.IR osinit (8),
.IR mkcard (10.1)
.br
.B /docs/BAREMETAL.md
.SH BUGS
One unit.
.br
Requests are made of the hardware a block at a time.
.br
A range may be named over another, or over the whole of a mounted file
system; the desktop's namespace has
.B #S
removed from it for that reason (see
.IR osinit (8)).
.SH HISTORY
The device of this name in Inferno's other native kernels serves a
directory per controller and unit
.RB ( /dev/sdC0/data ),
for ATA and SCSI discs, and parses partition tables itself.
This one shares the letter and the purpose and nothing else.
